      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14228956/assets/e6a6d3891e514f31c77ccbf098d06843bbcd771f2ebea83861683584ec5e8805.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is an ellipse. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif the axes pass through the opposite vertices of the ellipse.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14228956/assets/d06b0e6d93c3c0679aea11512963e19c2e641f506558f60c185624de2464c451.png)",
